+# Monarch’s Journey cross-version findings
+
+Date: 22 August 2026
+
+The repository’s four executable collections were reconstructed in temporary analysis storage and verified against their manifests. No decoded executable was retained in the repository.
+
+## Verified identities
+
+| Build | Size | SHA-256 |
+|---|---:|---|
+| Windows 3.3.2 | 24,727,272 | `83ba6a687270620633644bfdc93ecc2dd4c8579da66ee085e31af9aa0a506e75` |
+| Windows May 2020 3.3.3 | 24,753,368 | `656f4f482ed698958e1108938f7e5baff5b5dd31b3b310a7ea51386faca635d8` |
+| Windows 3.3.5.1 | 24,236,024 | `a0cc8e92287ac900b0552f5cca20df5acedf4773244923a77b15bcdbe143b13d` |
+| Linux May 2020 3.3.3 | 27,729,272 | `99776be0b9b72b06a83ac7606e8df553dfcec4b4ce25ee40c7d1961ea12791a6` |
+
+## Feature-component matrix
+
+| Component/evidence | Win 3.3.2 | Win May 3.3.3 | Linux May 3.3.3 | Win 3.3.5.1 |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| `red_king/ruler_feats` | yes | yes | yes | yes |
+| `common/monarchs_journey` string | no | yes | yes | yes |
+| `RULER_FEAT_LEVEL_` | yes | yes | yes | yes |
+| `FEAT_PROGRESS*` UI strings | yes | yes | yes | no |
+| `feat_progress_storage.cpp` | yes | yes | yes | yes |
+| `feat_progress` save key | yes | yes | yes | yes |
+| `can_see_highlighted_rulers` | no | yes | yes | no |
+| `highlighted_ruler_version` | yes | yes | yes | no |
+| `CNullGameSpark` symbols | no | stripped | exported | stripped |
+| local/null GameSparks implementation | not established | present | complete/reference | retained pieces |
+
+This confirms that 3.3.5.1 is not simply a build with different offsets: it retains low-level feat/progress material but loses several high-level Monarch’s Journey/UI initialization markers. Raw May 2020 offsets must not be copied to it.
+
+## Linux reference symbols
+
+The Linux executable retains dynamic symbols that identify the intended component boundaries:
+
+```text
+CFeatProgressStorage::Update()                 0x00f46722
+CFeatProgressStorage::ReadCachedProgress()     0x00f46958
+CFeatProgressStorage::SetNewProgress(...)      0x00f47220
+CFeatProgressStorage::ReadProgressFromKeyValueStorage() 0x00f4731a
+
+CRulerFeatTracker::IsActiveForPlaythrough()     0x00ff6d46
+CRulerFeatTracker::CalcShouldTrackFeatProgress() 0x00ff6e38
+CRulerFeatTracker::UpdateFeatProgress()        0x00ff78a6
+CRulerFeatTracker::RecalcScore()               0x00ff7cc2
+CRulerFeatTracker::ReloadFeatsDatabase()       0x00ff7f7a
+
+CNullGameSpark::LoadLocalCache()               0x01644f2a
+CNullGameSpark::ParseGameSparksData(...)       0x01644ffa
+CNullGameSpark::ParsePropertySetKeyData(...)   0x01645260
+CGameSparksInterface::AccessInstance()         0x01644e16
+CGameSparksInterface::CreateInstance(...)        0x01644e1e
+CGameSparksInterface::UninitialiseInstance()     0x01644e82
+```
+
+The Linux tracker flow is especially useful:
+
+1. `UpdateFeatProgress()` calls `CalcShouldTrackFeatProgress()`.
+2. If that returns false, it exits without evaluating feat scripts.
+3. Otherwise it evaluates the complete feat database for the current character.
+4. It writes changed progress through `CFeatProgressStorage::SetNewProgress()`.
+5. It recalculates the local challenge score.
+6. It calls `CFeatProgressStorage::Update()` to cache/persist progress.
+
+## Windows 3.3.3 matched functions
+
+Control-flow matching against the Linux reference identifies these Windows May 3.3.3 functions:
+
+```text
+CRulerFeatTracker::IsActiveForPlaythrough()       VA 0x1407b8370, raw 0x007b7770
+CRulerFeatTracker::CalcShouldTrackFeatProgress()  VA 0x1407b8450, raw 0x007b7850
+CRulerFeatTracker::UpdateFeatProgress()           VA 0x1407b8e60, raw 0x007b8260
+CRulerFeatTracker::RecalcScore()                 VA 0x1407b9340, raw 0x007b8740
+```
+
+The current V6 edit at raw `0x007b78eb` bypasses one later achievement/checksum-style predicate inside `CalcShouldTrackFeatProgress()`. It does not bypass the earlier `IsActiveForPlaythrough()` result at the start of that function.
+
+That distinction matches the V6 runtime symptom: a serialized value can be visible after loading while new feat evaluation never begins. Runtime tracing should confirm the return value of the active-playthrough check before another permanent patch is considered.
+
+## Next comparison work
+
+- Match the Windows 3.3.2/3.3.3/3.3.5.1 tracker functions by control-flow shape and constants rather than raw offsets.
+- Compare the Windows 3.3.5.1 initialization callers against the Linux/May Windows tracker entry points.
+- Use a debugger on the user’s exact V6/V7 executable to record whether `IsActiveForPlaythrough`, `CalcShouldTrackFeatProgress`, `UpdateFeatProgress`, and `RecalcScore` are reached.
+- Only then decide whether an additional guarded offline patch is justified.
